On Dec 17, 1:33 pm, Daniel Kraft <d...@domob.eu> wrote:
> Hi Michal,

> > 2. Break Nethack into pieces and assemble it into a modern
> > OS project, not only code wise, but in general.  By that I
> > mean community oriented, with a bugtracker, repository, beta
> > releases, etc.  Code wise that would mean translating into a
> > modern programming language like C++, Java, Python, D, etc
> > (most likely object-oriented).

> I'm pretty sure D and Java is out if you want to find some
> people here working with you;

Why?  D is a marginal language, at best, only available on a
very restricted set of platforms (and not on the one I usually
play on).  But Java is pretty much ubiquious, and it's
portability model seems eminently suited for something like
Nethack.  About the only question I'd have with regards to Java
is the user interface: I sort of like the ASCII in an xterm look
and feel, and I'm not sure how to get that with Java (short of
implementing my own text window with Swing---which may not be
that difficult).

> and I guess Python also (at least C++ would be the only one of
> those languages I'd want to work with for this project).
> Regarding C++, it's my opinion that this can make things much
> easier than plain C.

C++ would definitely be an improvement over C.  If you are only
targetting a closed set of compilers and systems, it would be my
first choice.  But portability still remains a serious problem
with C++; code which one compiler accepts will often be rejected
by another.  And by today's standards, Nethack is a pretty small
project, and it doesn't require a very high degree of
robustness, so most of Java's disadvantages don't come into
play.

> But if you really want to get some hackers onto your boat, my
> feeling is that staying with C would be the best choice.

I'd rather not see such hackers getting involved.  The main
goal, I thought, was to end up with a clean code base, and not a
bowl of spaghetti.

> Do you mean 2 to be something like "write a new rogue-like
> based on NetHack gameplay from scratch" or instead "start with
> NetHack sources, but bear in mind that the first projects are
> to rewrite it piece-wise"?

I doubt that a piece-wise rewrite would be practical.  There are
no real component barriers in the current code, and changing any
structure in the slightest means modifications in any number of
source files.

The real problem, if you want to do a rewrite, is getting a
concrete specification of what the current code does.  And
getting a good high level design to implement it.  The coding
(regardless of the language) is really a secondary issue, almost
mechanical.

